\begin{frame}{1. Introduction to Statistics}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Statistics} is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing,
interpreting and communicating data.
\item It converts observations into quantitative evidence for decision-making.
\item Two broad roles:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Descriptive statistics}: summarize observed data.
\item \textbf{Inferential statistics}: generalize from a sample to a population.
\end{enumerate}
\item In AI and Data Science, statistics supports experimentation,
feature analysis, uncertainty quantification and model evaluation.
\end{itemize}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 4
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Descriptive, Inferential and Frequentist Statistics}
\begin{block}{Descriptive statistics}
Describes the data actually observed using tables, graphs and numerical summaries.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Inferential statistics}
Uses sample information to make statements about an underlying population.
\end{block}
\begin{block}{Frequentist statistics}
Treats model parameters as fixed but unknown quantities and probability as
long-run frequency under repeated sampling.
\end{block}
\important{A confidence interval is a procedure with a long-run coverage property,
not a probability statement about a fixed parameter.}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 5
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Population, Sample, Parameter and Statistic}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Population}: complete set of units or outcomes of interest.
\item \textbf{Sample}: subset observed from the population.
\item \textbf{Parameter}: numerical characteristic of the population.
\item \textbf{Statistic}: numerical quantity calculated from the sample.
\end{itemize}
\[
\text{Population}\longrightarrow
\boxed{\text{Sampling}}\longrightarrow
\text{Sample}\longrightarrow
\boxed{\text{Estimation}}\longrightarrow
\text{Parameter}
\]
Examples:
\[
\mu=\text{population mean},\qquad
\bar X=\text{sample mean}.
\]
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 6
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Parameter versus Statistic}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Statistic}\\
\midrule
Refers to & Population & Sample\\
Typical notation & $\mu,\sigma^2,p$ & $\bar X,S^2,\hat p$\\
Usually known? & No & Computable\\
Role & Target quantity & Estimator/summary\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\vspace{.3cm}
\begin{itemize}
\item A statistic may be used as an \textbf{estimator} of a parameter.
\item Repeated samples produce different statistic values.
\item A parameter is fixed for a specified population/model.
\end{itemize}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 7
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Statistics in Data Science and AI}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{EDA}: identify central tendency, spread, skewness and anomalies.
\item \textbf{Feature engineering}: detect redundant or highly correlated variables.
\item \textbf{PCA}: reduce dimension using covariance structure.
\item \textbf{Machine learning}: estimate parameters and quantify prediction error.
\item \textbf{Experimentation}: compare treatments, models or algorithms.
\item \textbf{Anomaly detection}: identify observations inconsistent with the data.
\item \textbf{Uncertainty}: confidence intervals and sampling distributions
help communicate reliability.
\end{itemize}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 8
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Example: Statistical Analysis of Features}
Suppose a dataset contains student features:
\[
X_1=\text{study hours},\quad X_2=\text{attendance},\quad
X_3=\text{previous score}.
\]
A statistical workflow is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item inspect distributions with histograms;
\item calculate mean, median and standard deviation;
\item calculate skewness and kurtosis;
\item detect outliers using IQR;
\item calculate covariance/correlation;
\item construct the covariance matrix;
\item use PCA if dimensionality reduction is required.
\end{enumerate}
\important{The objective is not merely to calculate numbers, but to understand
the structure and uncertainty in the data.}
\end{frame}

