\begin{frame}{3. Skewness}
\textbf{Skewness} measures the degree and direction of asymmetry in a
probability distribution.

The population coefficient of skewness is commonly defined as
\[
\boxed{
\gamma_1=
\frac{\E[(X-\mu)^3]}{\sigma^3}
}
\]

For a sample, a simple moment-based estimate is
\[
\boxed{
g_1=
\frac{
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-\bar{x})^3
}{
\left[
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-\bar{x})^2
\right]^{3/2}
}
}
\]

\begin{itemize}
\item $\gamma_1=0$: symmetric distribution.
\item $\gamma_1>0$: \textbf{right-skewed}; longer right tail.
\item $\gamma_1<0$: \textbf{left-skewed}; longer left tail.
\end{itemize}

\important{Skewness is dimensionless and is based on the third central moment.}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 15 - NEW
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Skewness: Interpretation and Examples}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{.28\linewidth}p{.28\linewidth}p{.32\linewidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{Skewness} & \textbf{Typical Shape}\\
\midrule
Symmetric & $\approx 0$ & Balanced tails\\
Right-skewed & $>0$ & Long tail to the right\\
Left-skewed & $<0$ & Long tail to the left\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{.3cm}

For a moderately right-skewed distribution, the typical relationship is
\[
\boxed{\text{Mean}>\text{Median}>\text{Mode}}
\]

For a moderately left-skewed distribution:
\[
\boxed{\text{Mean}<\text{Median}<\text{Mode}}
\]

Examples:
\begin{itemize}
\item Income distributions are often right-skewed.
\item Exam scores may be left-skewed when most students score highly.
\item Skewness helps determine whether mean-based summaries adequately
represent a dataset.
\end{itemize}

\important{The mean--median--mode relationship is a useful rule of thumb,
not a universal mathematical rule for every distribution.}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 16 - NEW
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{4. Kurtosis}
\textbf{Kurtosis} describes the heaviness of the tails of a distribution
relative to a reference distribution.

The population kurtosis is the standardized fourth central moment:
\[
\boxed{
\beta_2=
\frac{\E[(X-\mu)^4]}{\sigma^4}
}
\]

The commonly reported \textbf{excess kurtosis} is
\[
\boxed{
\gamma_2=\beta_2-3
}
\]

For a normal distribution:
\[
\beta_2=3,\qquad \gamma_2=0.
\]

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Mesokurtic}: excess kurtosis $\approx0$.
\item \textbf{Leptokurtic}: positive excess kurtosis; heavier tails.
\item \textbf{Platykurtic}: negative excess kurtosis; lighter tails.
\end{itemize}

\important{Kurtosis is especially useful for identifying distributions
with unusually heavy or light tails and potential extreme observations.}
\end{frame}

% ============================================================
% 17 - NEW
% ============================================================
\begin{frame}{Kurtosis: Interpretation and Comparison}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{p{.28\linewidth}p{.25\linewidth}p{.35\linewidth}}
\toprule
\textbf{Distribution} & \textbf{Excess Kurtosis} & \textbf{Interpretation}\\
\midrule
Mesokurtic & $\approx0$ & Similar tail weight to normal\\
Leptokurtic & $>0$ & Heavier tails; more extreme values\\
Platykurtic & $<0$ & Lighter tails; fewer extreme values\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{.3cm}

Kurtosis is based on the fourth central moment:
\[
\gamma_2=
\frac{\E[(X-\mu)^4]}{\sigma^4}-3.
\]

\begin{itemize}
\item The fourth power gives greater weight to observations far from the mean.
\item Therefore, extreme observations can strongly affect kurtosis.
\item Kurtosis does \textbf{not} simply measure the height or sharpness of
the central peak.
\end{itemize}

\important{In practical data analysis, skewness and kurtosis together provide
useful information about asymmetry and tail behavior.}
\end{frame}

